#%PAM-1.0
#
# PAM stack for the login screen's own logind session — ADR 0037 §决策 6,
# installed by packaging/install.sh as /etc/pam.d/inox-greeter.
#
# Who loads it: systemd, not inox-greet. `PAMName=inox-greeter` in
# packaging/systemd/inox-greeter@.service makes systemd open a session under this
# name for the unit, as the `greeter` account. systemd opens the session; it never
# authenticates, which is why `auth` below permits unconditionally.
#
# What this session buys: one logind session that owns the greeter's VT, with
# XDG_SESSION_ID and /run/user/<greeter uid> in the unit's environment. libseat's
# logind backend takes the DRM device and the input devices from that session, so
# inox-host can start inox-compositor without inox-compositor ever touching a VT
# ioctl or a DRM fd it was handed (ADR 0037 §决策 1).
#
# The VT itself comes from the unit: `TTYPath=/dev/tty%i` plus the explicit
# `Environment=XDG_VTNR=%i`. systemd does not put XDG_VTNR into a tty unit's
# environment on its own (verified on Arch with `getty@tty3.service`: its
# Environment= is empty), and pam_systemd reads the VT from the process
# environment. Without that variable the greeter's session has no VT, libseat gets
# no seat, and the compositor never reaches the display.
#
# No password is ever checked here: the greeter has no way to log anybody in
# (inox-greet links inox-auth with `default-features = false`, so it has no PAM and
# no session-starting code), and this session belongs to a system account whose
# shell is nologin.
#
# `account` and `session` go through system-login for the same reasons as the login
# stack, plus pam_env (so the greeter gets a sane PATH/HOME) and pam_limits.

auth       optional   pam_permit.so
account    include    system-login
session    include    system-login
